Topic 4. Malicious Updates

Overview: The core idea. We’re always told to keep systems patched, and that’s still right. But every update is really new software being installed on your machine, usually with high privileges and often automatically. If an attacker can tamper with that update, they get their code run on your system with your full trust. It’s RCE delivered through the front door.

Two main ways it happens:
The fake update. You visit a dodgy website and a pop-up says “Your Flash/browser/video player is out of date, click to update.” It’s not a real update at all, just malware dressed up as one. This one is easy to fall for but also easy to avoid.
The poisoned real update. Much scarier. The attacker breaks into the software company itself and slips malicious code into a genuine update. Everything checks out: it comes from the right server and carries the right digital signature. That’s SolarWinds (December 2020), which Messer uses as his example.

2.3 #4 Malicious Updates

We’re always told to keep our systems updated, and that’s still good advice. But every update is really new software being installed on your device, often automatically and with high-level permissions. If an attacker can tamper with an update, the device will happily run their code because it trusts the source.

There are two main types.

The fake update. A pop-up on a dodgy website or app tells you something is out of date, your phone needs “cleaning”, or you’re about to lose your data. It isn’t a real update at all, just malware dressed up as one. This is often called scareware, because it uses fear and urgency to rush you into clicking before you’ve had time to think.

I’ve seen this first-hand. Someone I help look after got a new phone and kept seeing pop-ups warning that he needed to clean up his phone or he’d lose all his photos and contacts. The photos he was worried about were actually on Facebook, not on the phone, so there was nothing to lose. But the fear did its job and he clicked to download it. After that the phone got stuck in a loop: every few minutes it made a loud beeping noise and the same prompt came back up, over and over. Resolved by  fulll factory reset. It’s a good example of how scareware doesn’t need to be clever. It just needs to make you panic.

The poisoned real update. This is the scary one. Attackers break into the software company itself and slip malicious code into a genuine update. The best-known example is SolarWinds (December 2020), where a backdoor was hidden in a legitimate, digitally signed update that went out to thousands of organisations, including US government agencies.

Why it’s harder to defend against than phishing. A phishing email usually leaves clues: a misspelt domain, odd headers, a skewed logo, wording that doesn’t sound right. You can learn to spot those. A poisoned real update has no clues. It comes from the genuine server, with a valid digital signature, at the time you’d expect it, and sometimes you have no choice, because the app won’t work until you update. A digital signature proves who sent the update, not whether it’s safe. If the sender has been compromised, the signature means nothing. And one poisoned update reaches every customer at once, not one person at a time.

Defences

Only update from official sources (the developer’s own site or the built-in updater), never from a pop-up
Check digital signatures and hashes where possible
Test updates before rolling them out to everyone (staged rollouts, from 1.3 change management)
Watch for unusual behaviour after an update, such as a trusted program suddenly contacting a strange server. This is where SOC monitoring comes in.
Defences to listen for:

Get updates only from the official source (the developer’s own site or the built-in updater), never from a pop-up on some other website
Check digital signatures (most modern operating systems require signed software before installing)
Test before rolling out to everyone (your staged rollouts from 1.3 change management)
Watch for odd behaviour after an update, because a poisoned signed update can only be caught by what it does

The thing to think about while watching: a digital signature proves who sent the update, not whether it’s safe. If the sender has been compromised, the signature means nothing.
