a phishing email usually leaves clues, and you’ve learned to spot them (sender headers, misspelt domains, skewed logos, odd wording). Now push it one step further, because this is the bit that makes SolarWinds so scary:

A poisoned real update has no clues at all. It comes from the genuine server and the genuine domain, with a valid digital signature, at the time you’d expect an update. Every check you learned in those TryHackMe rooms passes. So it’s not just harder to detect. For the person on the receiving end, it’s basically impossible to detect.

That changes who does the defending:

Phishing: the user is a real line of defence. Training works, because people can learn the red flags.
Poisoned updates: the user can’t help, so defence has to happen elsewhere. The software company has to secure its build systems. The organisation tests updates before rolling them out widely. Security teams watch for unusual behaviour afterwards, like a trusted program suddenly contacting a strange server. That last one is SOC work: SolarWinds was eventually caught by a security company noticing odd activity, not by anyone inspecting the update.

There’s also the scale problem. A phishing email tricks one person at a time. One poisoned update goes to every customer at once, which is how SolarWinds reached thousands of organisations.
