Topic 8: Hardware 2.3
Software problems can usually be fixed with an update. Hardware problems are harder, because the flaw is built into the physical device or the low-level code that runs it, and sometimes the manufacturer has stopped caring about it altogether.

The analogy: your house
Firmware is the wiring inside the walls.
End-of-life is when the builder stops building that style of house.
Legacy is the old house still standing that nobody wants to renovate.
1. Firmware

Firmware is the software built into hardware: the code that tells a router, printer, BIOS, hard drive or smart device how to work. It sits below the operating system.
The problems:

It’s often forgotten. People patch Windows but never update the router or printer firmware.
The manufacturer controls it. If they don’t release a fix, you can’t patch it yourself.
It’s hard to detect. Malware hiding in firmware can survive a complete reinstall of the operating system.

That’s like faulty wiring inside the walls. You can repaint every room (reinstall the OS), but the problem is still in the walls, and only an electrician (the manufacturer) can fix it properly.

A famous example is Spectre and Meltdown in 2018: flaws in the design of the processors themselves, affecting nearly every computer made in the previous 20 years. They needed firmware and OS patches as a workaround, because you can’t patch a chip’s physical design.

2. End-of-life (EOL) and end-of-service-life (EOSL)

These are two stages:

End-of-life (EOL): the manufacturer stops selling the product. It may still get support and patches for a while.
End-of-service-life (EOSL): the manufacturer stops supporting it. There are no more security patches or fixes.

EOSL is the dangerous one. Any vulnerability found after that date stays open forever, and attackers know it.

In car terms, EOL is when the dealer stops selling that model. EOSL is when there are no more recalls, parts or servicing. The car still runs, but if the brakes have a known fault, nobody’s coming to fix it.

3. Legacy platforms

Legacy systems are old hardware or software still in use after support has ended, usually because:

Replacing them is expensive
Critical software only runs on the old system
“It still works, so why change it?”

I know this one from Medical Imaging Australia. Medical and imaging equipment is a classic example. A scanner that cost a fortune is often tied to an old version of Windows, and the vendor certifies the machine only with that exact setup. The hospital can’t just upgrade the OS without breaking the equipment or its certification, so the old system stays in use. This machine that was old should have been replaced years ago and never even attempt to do a OS rollout.

How organisations deal with it

When you can’t patch or replace, you reduce the risk around it:

Update firmware regularly, and include it in patch management, not just the OS.
Track EOL/EOSL dates in asset management so replacements are planned before support ends.
Isolate legacy systems with network segmentation. Put the old scanner on its own network segment with no internet access, so even if it’s vulnerable, attackers can’t easily reach it.
Compensating controls: extra monitoring, strict access control and firewalls around the weak system.
Replace when possible.

Back to the house: if you can’t rewire, you lock the door to that room, put a smoke alarm in it, and don’t let anyone plug a heater into it.
EOL vs. EOSL for Security+
EOL — End of Life	EOSL — End of Service Life
What stops	Production and sale of the product	All vendor support: patches, updates, help desk, spare parts
Patches?	May still be available (sometimes paid/extended)	No. Every new CVE after this date is permanent
Security meaning	A warning — start planning replacement	Reality — you have no patch path; exposure is unbounded
Analogy	The store stopped stocking the product, but the manufacturer will still answer your phone for a while	The manufacturer deleted your number from their system

Timeline: EOL comes first, EOSL comes after (typically 2–5 years later). 

How the exam wants you to think about it
A question that says "vendor no longer sells the product" → EOL
A question that says "no longer receives security patches or vendor support" → EOSL
The security risk question: the dangerous date is EOSL, because that's when the patch path is permanently gone.  EOL is the planning window; EOSL is the exposure window.
Exam tip: If a scenario describes a system that "still works but will never be patched again," the answer is EOSL, not EOL. The product is still running — it just has no safety net. 
