CompTIA Security+ 2.3: Hardware Vulnerabilities

Software problems can usually be fixed with an update. Hardware problems are harder, because the flaw is built into the physical device or the low-level code that runs it, and sometimes the manufacturer has stopped caring about it altogether.

A simple way to picture it is a house:

Firmware is the wiring inside the walls.
End-of-life is when the builder stops building that style of house.
Legacy is the old house still standing that nobody wants to renovate.
Firmware

Firmware is the software built into hardware: the code that tells a laptop’s BIOS/UEFI, a router, a printer or a hard drive how to work. It sits below the operating system, which is why it’s so often overlooked.

Part of the problem is that responsibility is split. On a Windows laptop, Microsoft patches the operating system through Windows Update, which most organisations manage well. But the firmware is the responsibility of the hardware maker (HP, Dell, Lenovo) or the chip maker (Intel, AMD), and it’s often delivered through their own separate tools. Because it’s a separate patching channel, it gets missed. Firmware updates are also riskier, because a failed update can brick the device, so admins tend to put them off.

Faulty firmware is like faulty wiring inside the walls. You can repaint every room (reinstall the operating system), but the problem is still there, and only the electrician (the manufacturer) can fix it properly.

End-of-life (EOL) vs end-of-service-life (EOSL)
EOL: the vendor stops selling the product. It may still get security patches for a while, but it’s a warning that support is ending soon.
EOSL: the vendor stops supporting the product. No more security patches, no more fixes. Any vulnerability found after this date stays open forever.
Legacy systems

A legacy system is any outdated hardware or software still in use, usually because it’s expensive to replace, critical software only runs on it, or “it still works, so why change it?” A system can be legacy before it reaches EOSL, but once a legacy system is also past EOSL, it’s in a dangerous state.

A real-world example: the orange payroll computer

When I worked as a field technician for a medical imaging company, I came across a computer at one of the clinics that ran their payroll. It was clearly from the 1980s, and this was in the 2000s. It had been white once, but it had aged to orange. It was definitely legacy, and long past any vendor support.

Nobody seemed to know much about it. The clinic knew it did the payroll, and I only ever dealt with one staff member who used it. Headquarters couldn’t tell me anything about it either. When I backed it up, I had a feeling there were applications on it I didn’t know about and couldn’t back up. I told them to make sure everything it did was backed up, because if it died, I didn’t think it would come back.

It was a security problem, but it was also an every-week payroll problem. The machine was doing its job, so no one had thought about what would happen if it stopped. Coffee, a dead hard drive or an attacker would all lead to the same result: no payroll.

In security terms, this computer was a single point of failure, and the risk wasn’t just to confidentiality or integrity but to availability, the A in the CIA triad. The organisation had decades to plan for it. Setting up the payroll on a second machine, or having a documented recovery plan, wouldn’t have been expensive with that much warning, and the company easily had the budget.

How to manage hardware vulnerabilities

When you can’t patch or replace something straight away, you reduce the risk around it:

Include firmware in patch management, not just the operating system.
Track EOL and EOSL dates in asset management, so replacements are planned before support ends.
Keep an asset inventory. You can’t protect, back up or replace something nobody has documented.
Isolate legacy systems by taking them off the network or segmenting them, with firewalls around them. This doesn’t fix the vulnerability, but it makes it much harder for an attacker to reach.
Limit who uses them. Fewer users means a smaller attack surface.
Build in redundancy and continuity plans, so critical work like payroll can continue on another system if the old one fails.
Replace the system when possible.
