Topic 6. 2.3 Cloud-Specific Vulnerabilities.
The big idea: the shared responsibility model. Cloud is like renting a unit in a big storage facility. The facility (AWS, Azure, Google) is responsible for the fence, the gate, the security guards and the building itself. You’re responsible for your own unit: locking the roller door, deciding who gets a key, and what you leave inside. Most cloud breaches don’t happen because the facility gets broken into. They happen because a customer left their own roller door open.

The main vulnerabilities:

Misconfiguration, the number one cause. The classic example is a storage bucket (like Amazon S3) accidentally set to public, so anyone on the internet can browse the files. There have been many real breaches exactly like this, with millions of customer records sitting open. That’s the roller door left up.
Weak identity and access management. Accounts with far more permissions than they need, no MFA, or leaked access keys. A really common one: developers accidentally upload their cloud keys to GitHub, and bots scan GitHub constantly looking for them. Keep that in mind with your own GitHub. That’s handing a copy of your unit key to a stranger.
It’s publicly accessible by design. Cloud services are built to be reached from anywhere, so the attack surface is huge. That opens the door to DDoS and to attackers probing for normal application flaws: authentication bypass (getting in without logging in properly), directory traversal (wandering into folders you shouldn’t see), and remote code execution (running your own commands on their server).
Shared tenancy. You’re sharing physical hardware with strangers, so everything you just learned applies: VM escape and resource reuse are cloud risks too. That’s your link back to the last topic.

OPSEC: what I’m careful not to share ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

While studying cloud vulnerabilities, something clicked for me. Misconfiguration is one of the biggest causes of breaches, and the same goes for what we share publicly. This blog is public, and anyone can read it, including attackers. They do their research too.

Imagine someone writing a post like: “I’ve set up a new computer, haven’t patched it, haven’t changed any of the default settings, and can’t remember where i put it.” That’s like putting a sign on your front gate saying the door has no lock, there’s no alarm, and you’re never home. It’s an open invitation.

In security this is called OPSEC (operational security): being careful about what you reveal, because small details can add up to a picture an attacker can use.

So these are my rules for this blog:

I’ll write about my lab after I’ve secured it, or keep it general
I’ll never post IP addresses, usernames, hostnames, passwords, keys or details of my home network
I’ll check every screenshot before posting, because details hide in the corners: terminal prompts, browser tabs, file paths

Sharing what I’m learning is the whole point of this blog. But just like a hotel room, it’s my job to make sure I’m not leaving my access card out the front.

What the shared responsibility model is
The landlord analogy:

You rent an apartment in a secure building.
The landlord (cloud provider) takes care of the building — the structure, the front gate, the security cameras in the lobby, the plumbing, the power.
You (the customer) take care of your apartment — who you let in, whether you lock your door, what you leave on the windowsill. 
The building is safe. Your apartment is only as safe as you make it.

That's the entire model: the provider secures the cloud; you secure what's in it. 

The one-line version the exam uses
Security OF the cloud = provider's job (hardware, data center, physical security, hypervisor)
Security IN the cloud = your job (data, identities, access controls, application config) 
AWS secured the data center, the hardware, the network — the "building" was fine. But Capital One misconfigured their own WAF and IAM rules — they left their "apartment door" unlocked.  The breach was 100% on the customer's side of the line.

Exam tip: If a scenario says "the cloud provider's infrastructure was secure but the customer's bucket was public" or "the provider patched the hypervisor but the customer didn't patch the guest OS," the answer is shared responsibility — the breach was on the customer's side of the line, not the provider's. 

Why misconfiguration causes more cloud breaches than the cloud provider getting hacked
1. The provider's side is a small, hardened target
AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud have thousands of security engineers, bug bounties, penetration testing, redundant controls, and decades of operational hardening. A full hypervisor or data-center compromise is essentially unheard of — it would be a global news event.

2. The customer's side is enormous and constantly changing
A single enterprise might have thousands of resources across multiple accounts, regions, and services — each one a configuration decision. Every new deployment, migration, or "quick test" is a chance to leave a door open. An average account has ~43 misconfigurations at any given time (SentinelOne, 2024). 

3. Misconfigurations don't require a clever exploit
This is the key point. A provider hack requires a zero-day or novel technique — rare, expensive, and hard to pull off. A misconfiguration just requires a scanner:

Public S3 bucket? → curl it
Open security group on port 3389? → RDP in
Overly permissive IAM role? → aws sts assume-role. Lots of places not using MFA/best practices. 
Attackers automate scanning for these. They don't need to break anything — they just need to find what you already left open. 

4. It's a human problem, not a technology problem
82% of misconfigurations are caused by human error, not software flaws (SentinelOne).  The cloud is powerful because it's highly configurable — and that same power means one wrong checkbox, one "temporarily" public bucket, one default password, and you're exposed. 

exam answer
The provider's infrastructure is a small, professionally hardened, redundant target that has essentially never been fully breached. The customer's configuration surface is large, constantly changing, and set by humans who make mistakes — and misconfigurations don't require an exploit, just a scan. That's why the "building" is safe but the "apartment door" is left unlocked.

Exam tip: If a question asks "why do most cloud breaches happen?" and one answer is "customer misconfiguration" and another is "provider vulnerability," the answer is customer misconfiguration — always. 

One way leaked keys could happen, and how you’d stop it happening to you?

The sticky note on the front door
You move into a new house. Your alarm code is 4-7-2-9. You write it on a sticky note and stick it to the front door so you don't forget.

That's your .env file. It's just a text file that holds your secrets — API keys, passwords, tokens. Locally, on your machine, it's fine. It's in your pocket.
But then you push to GitHub. That's taking the sticky note, peeling it off the front door, and taping it to the outside of your front window for the whole street to see.

Now everyone walking past can read it. And here's the part that catches people out:

"Oh no, I'll just peel it off and push again."

No. The whole street already saw it. And even worse — the tape residue is still there. That's git history. You deleted the file, but anyone who looks at the old commits can still read 4-7-2-9.

How to stop it
Layer	What to do
Prevention	Add .env to .gitignore before the first commit. Never hardcode keys in source.
Secret storage	Use a secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, HashiCorp Vault) or at minimum environment variables injected at deploy time — not a file in your repo
Detection	Enable secret scanning (GitHub's built-in, or GitGuardian/TruffleHog in CI) — it blocks the push or alerts you before the key is public
Git hooks	Add a pre-commit hook (e.g. gitleaks or detect-secrets) that scans staged files and rejects the commit if a pattern looks like a key
Rotation	If a key does leak, rotate it immediately. Assume it's compromised the moment it's public.
Least privilege	The key you accidentally commit should only have the minimum permissions it needs — not admin. If it's a read-only S3 key for one bucket, the blast radius is small.

Managed identities	The real fix is use IAM roles / managed identities instead of static keys entirely. The cloud platform handles credentials automatically — there's nothing to leak.
With use an IAM (is like the bouncer) role instead of a static key, instead of writing down your house code on a sticky note, you get a temporary keycard from the bouncer
every morning that expires at midnight. There's no permanent key to steal, leak, or commit to a repo. The bouncer (IAM) hands you a new one each time you need it.

How it happens: You write the key down (in a file, in code, in a config) and it ends up somewhere public. The "left it on the window" part.
How to stop it: Don't write it down in the first place (secrets manager / IAM role), and if you did, catch it before it leaves your machine (pre-commit hook / secret scanning).
1. Deleting it doesn’t fix it. You said they can pull it down, and that’s the trap. Git keeps history, so the key is still sitting in old commits even after you delete it from the code. Worse, bots scan GitHub constantly, and leaked keys can be found and used within minutes. The only real fix is to revoke (rotate) the key so it stops working, then issue a new one. Hotel version: if you’ve lost your access card, you don’t just hope nobody finds it. You go to reception and get it cancelled.

 The proper terms for “don’t store keys in code Secrets management. keep keys in environment variables or a dedicated vault (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault), never in the code
.gitignore: a file that tells Git which files never to upload, such as the file holding your keys
Secret scanning: GitHub can automatically spot keys and block the push before it goes public
Least privilege: give each key only the permissions it needs, so a leaked key can do less damage


FOR THE EXAM: Keys get leaked when they’re hardcoded and pushed to a public repo. Prevent it with secrets management and secret scanning. If it happens, revoke the key immediately, because deleting it doesn’t remove it from history.

if it's a multiple choice, the answer they're looking for is almost always secrets management (don't store keys in plaintext) or key rotation (if it's already leaked, kill it). "Don't write it down" = use a secrets manager. "Catch it before" = secret scanning / pre-commit hooks.

The most common key leak is accidental commit to version control.  The primary prevention is secrets management + pre-commit scanning; the primary detection is automated secret scanning on repositories; the primary response is immediate rotation.
