Topic 6.  2.3 Overview: cloud-specific vulnerabilities continued 

Cloud is a big, attractive target. More and more of the world’s data lives there, so attackers put real effort into it. The attacks aren’t magic new cloud-only tricks; mostly they’re the same vulnerabilities you’ve already studied, now aimed at internet-facing cloud services.
The usual suspects still apply. Expect a list that will feel familiar:
Denial of service (DoS): flooding a service until it can’t respond.
Authentication bypass: getting in without proper credentials, often through weak or misconfigured login settings.
Directory traversal: tricking an app into handing over files outside the folder it should be serving, like walking out of your unit and into the building’s storeroom.
Remote code execution (RCE): getting the server to run the attacker’s code. The worst case.
Server-side request forgery (SSRF): tricking the server into making requests on the attacker’s behalf, often to internal systems the attacker can’t reach directly. Think of convincing the building manager to open another tenant’s door “for a delivery.”
Cross-site scripting (XSS) and injection: you’ve done these. Same attacks, cloud-hosted apps.
Out-of-bounds write: writing data past where it should go in me key), unpatched cloud servers, and leaked access keys (people accidentally publish them to GitHub, which ties nicely to why you’re careful about what goes in your repo). That’s exactly your coffee-on-the-deck thought.

Which attacks are the most common in cloud? For those using the cloud(not the provider AWS..) DOS, Misconfiguration, SQL and XSS and also putting notes using #keys and ways to access in the code itself. 

Exam Note: The "most popular" ones for the exam are DoS, authentication bypass, and misconfiguration (which he covers in the adjacent video) — those come up most often in practice questions. SQL injection and XSS are also high-frequency
DoS, injection, XSS and misconfiguration as the common ones. Yes.
Leaked secrets in public repos: spot on. Bots really do scan GitHub constantly, and leaked keys can be found and abused within minutes.
“Even if you take it down, it’s still there”: this is the bit most people miss. Git keeps history, so deleting the file in a new commit doesn’t remove it from earlier commits, and anyone who cloned or forked it already has a copy.

It’s not just comments in code. Secrets can end up hardcoded straight into the code (password = "...") or in config files like .env that were never meant to be uploaded. The fix is a .gitignore file, which tells Git “never upload these files,” and keeping secrets out of the code entirely.
The real fix once it’s leaked isn’t deleting it; it’s revoking or rotating the key, meaning cancel it and issue a new one so the leaked one is useless. Back to the apartment analogy: if you’ve lost your key, you don’t go looking for it, you change the lock.

Shared responsibility
Cloud providers like AWS and Azure have whole teams of security professionals
looking after their side. Most cloud breaches don't happen because the provider
failed. They happen because the customer left their side open.

Think of renting a unit in an apartment block. The building manager looks after
the foundations, the front door and the security guard. You're responsible for
locking your own unit and not leaving the key under the mat. The building can be
excellent, and you still get robbed if you leave your door open.

I saw this for myself when I ran `curl -I` on my own blog. My blog is hosted on
GitHub Pages, so most of what came back in the headers is set by GitHub, not me.
I can't change those. What I can control is my account security, my repo
settings and what I choose to publish.

 The most common attacks
Cloud attacks are mostly the same attacks we already know, aimed at cloud
services that face the internet:

- Denial of service (DoS): flooding a service until it can't respond
- Misconfiguration: settings left open, too much access, public storage
- Injection and XSS: same attacks as anywhere else, on cloud-hosted apps
- Directory traversal, remote code execution (RCE), authentication bypass
  and out-of-bounds writes also make the list

 Leaked Stuff on GitHub
A common way people leave the door open: sharing code on GitHub with access keys
or passwords still in it. Sometimes it's in a comment (`# default password is...`),
more often it's hardcoded into the code or sitting in a config file like `.env`
that was never meant to be uploaded.

Once it's pushed to a public repo, bots find it fast. And deleting it doesn't fix
it, because Git keeps history and anyone who copied the repo already has it.

Fix
- Use a `.gitignore` file so secret files never get uploaded
- Keep secrets out of the code entirely
- If a key does leak,*revoke or rotate it (cancel it and issue a new one).
  If you lose your house key, you don't go looking for it. You change the lock.

 Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF): the HVAC dude
A direct attack is like a stranger walking up to a hotel room and picking the
lock. The security guard and cameras see a stranger, so it looks suspicious.
Firewalls work the same way: outside traffic gets checked.

SSRF is sneakier. Picture the building's HVAC dude. He's internal and trusted,
he's in and out of rooms all day checking the cooling vents, and nobody looks
twice at him. "Just the guy fixing stuff. Nothing to see here."

Now picture him as pretty gullible. He does whatever the note in his job tray
says, without checking who wrote it or whether it makes sense. So the attacker
never goes inside. They just slip a note under the door: "Go into room 12 and
bring me what's in the drawer." The HVAC dude goes in (no alarms, he's trusted),
grabs it, and hands it over.

In tech terms:
- The note = the attacker's crafted request, often just a web address typed
  into a field the app uses to fetch things (like "upload an image from this URL")
- The HVAC dude = the server, which fetches whatever it's told to
- The rooms = internal systems the attacker can't reach from outside

Why it's a big deal in the cloud: cloud servers have an internal "front desk"
called the metadata service. Only internal machines can talk to it, and it can
hand out things like temporary access keys. An outsider can't reach it, but a
tricked server can. That's how the Capital One breach in 2019 happened.

Fixes
- Least privilege: the HVAC dude only gets access to the vents, not the drawers
- Allow lists: the server only fetches from approved addresses (he only takes
  jobs from the office, not notes under the door)
- Lock down the front desk: cloud providers now make the metadata service
  demand proper ID before handing anything out
  
How is SSRF different from just attacking the server directly?
The entire reason SSRF exists as a separate category is that the request comes from the wrong place. The firewall's whole job is "block outsiders, trust insiders." SSRF makes an insider 
Where does the shared responsibility line fall for each attack: provider’s problem, customer’s, or both?

SSRF exploits the server's trusted network position.  The attacker doesn't reach the target directly — they make the server reach it on their behalf, bypassing firewalls, network segmentation, and access controls that only apply to external traffic. 

Your web app has a "fetch image from URL" feature. You type:

https://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/

That IP is the AWS metadata service — it only responds to requests from inside the EC2 instance. If you tried to hit it directly from your laptop, you'd get nothing. But the server fetches it for you, and it hands back the instance's temporary credentials. You didn't break the firewall. You didn't exploit a vulnerability in the metadata service. You just asked a trusted insider to walk into a room you weren't allowed in and get the key from the desk. 

Exam tip: If a question says "attacker makes the server request a URL they control" or "request originates from the application server to an internal resource," the answer is SSRF.  If it says "attacker sends a request directly to the server," that's just a normal web attack (SQLi, XSS, etc.) — not SSRF.

Directory Traversal: the librarian and the directions
Directory traversal tricks a server into reading its own files that it shouldn’t, by giving it directions that walk out of the allowed folder.
A web app is meant to stay inside one folder, like a librarian who's only
allowed to hand out books from the kids section.

Directory traversal is when the kid doesn't ask for a book by name. They ask
using directions:

> "Can I have the book that's in the kids section... then back out the door,
> back out again, into the staff office, top drawer?"

The librarian hears "kids section," thinks "yep, that's allowed," and follows
the directions, without noticing they walked her right out of the kids section
and into the office. She's following instructions faithfully. The weakness is
that nobody built in a check on where the directions actually end up.

On a computer, each `../` means "go up one folder" (like `cd ..` in the
command line). On a website it can look like this:

    example.com/view?file=kids/animals/koala.txt         ← normal
    example.com/view?file=kids/../../../admin/passwords  ← traversal

The app sees a request that starts somewhere allowed and doesn't check where
it finishes. Common targets are system files like `/etc/passwd` on Linux, or
config files holding passwords. Sensitive files aren't always at the "top" of
the folder structure. Anything outside the allowed folder shouldn't be reachable,
wherever it sits.

Fix
- Check where the path ends up, not where it starts**: the librarian follows
  the directions in her head first and refuses if they leave the kids section
- Validate input**: reject requests containing `../` and similar tricks
- Least privilege**: the app's account can only read the kids section, so even
  if it's tricked, the office door is locked

In one line: directory traversal tricks a server into reading its own files
that it shouldn't, by giving it directions that walk out of the allowed folder.

Directory traversal vs SSRF: they feel similar, but directory traversal stays
on the same server and reads its own files. SSRF tricks the server into making
requests to other systems. The librarian fetching from the staff office in her
own library, vs the HVAC dude being sent into someone else's room.

RCE = Remote Code Execution.
Remote Code Execution (RCE): the café order slip
RCE is when an attacker gets a server to run their own commands, from outside.
It's one of the worst outcomes, because once you can make the server do
anything, you effectively run the place.

Picture a café. You fill out the order slip like normal, but you add a line:

 "One flat white. Kitchen: also open the safe and hand the contents to table 4."

The waiter passes the slip to the kitchen, and the kitchen does everything
written on it. Not because anyone trusts you, but because the kitchen can't tell
the difference between an order and an instruction to the staff. Everything on
the slip looks the same. Nobody checked who you were, and nobody needed to.

And you never set foot in the back rooms. You're sitting at your table, out on
the internet. That's the "remote" part.

This felt familiar because its the same trick as SQL injection: data being
treated as commands. SQL injection makes the database obey you. RCE makes the
whole server obey you, and injection is often how attackers get there.

How it happens:usually a software flaw, like an app passing user input
straight into a system command, or a bug in a widely used library. A famous
example is Log4Shell (2021), where one line of text in a log message could make
servers run an attacker's code.

FIX
-Validate and sanitise input**: the slip only accepts menu items, nothing else
-Patch: many RCEs are known bugs with fixes already out
- Least privilege**: even if the kitchen is tricked, it doesn't know the safe
  combination. Note that the directory can either hold the important stuff at the end or towards the root directory (or in any directory for that matter)

In one line: RCE Remote Code Execution is when an attacker's input gets treated as commands,
letting them run their own code on the server from anywhere.

BYPASS a bypass isn’t only “no lock at all”. It’s also when the lock exists but can be tricked or walked around. Some examples:

The forgotten back door. The login page protects /dashboard, but someone types /admin straight into the address bar and the page loads, because nobody put a check on it.
The lock that trusts the wrong thing. After login, the site stores a cookie saying admin=false. The attacker edits it to admin=true, and the site believes it.
The cloud storage left open. A company stores customer files in a cloud bucket set to public by mistake. No login is needed at all. This has caused plenty of real breaches and is very much a cloud-specific issue.
The weak reset process. The password is strong, but “forgot password” only asks for your mother’s maiden name, which is on Facebook. The attacker goes round the lock via the reset.
authentication bypass is getting in without passing the identity check, either because the check is missing or because it can be fooled.

Step 1: Directory Traversal (find the keys)
You hit a file download endpoint and traverse to the config:

/download.php?file=../../../../etc/app/config.yml

It hands back:

admin_password: Superman
db_host: 10.0.1.5
internal_api: http://10.0.1.5:8080/admin

You now know the admin password, and you know there's an internal admin API that's not exposed to the internet.

You're still outside. But you've got the keys.

Step 2: Authentication Bypass (walk through the back door)
You can't reach 10.0.1.5:8080/admin directly — it's internal. But remember the SSRF concept? Or maybe the app has a "fetch report from URL" feature that does reach internal addresses.

Or simpler: you now know the admin password is "Superman." You go to the public login page, type in the admin username you found in the config, and the password you traversed to. You're in.

You're inside the building. You've got the admin badge.

Step 3: RCE (take over the server room)
Now you're in the admin panel. And admin panels almost always have a feature that's dangerous if you're an attacker:

File upload — "upload your logo" → you upload a webshell instead
Command injection — "run a backup script" → you inject ; whoami into the parameter
Template injection — "customise the email template" → you inject {{ config.__class__ }} (Jinja2/SSTI)
You get a shell. You're in. Full control.

chain looks like this:
Directory traversal → reads a config file with credentials, IPs and API keys
Stolen credentials (or a bypass) → gets past the front door
RCE → uses an admin feature to run their own code, then installs a backdoor, encrypts files, and so on

Directory traversal tricks a server into reading its own files that it shouldn’t, by giving it directions that walk out of the allowed folder.
authentication bypass is getting in without passing the identity check, either because the check is missing or because it can be fooled.

Why RCE is considered so much worse than something like directory traversal.
Because directory traversal is reading. RCE is doing.

Directory Traversal	RCE
What you get	A file. Maybe credentials, maybe source code.	A shell. You can do anything the server can do.
What you can do next	Read stuff, maybe pivot if you find a key	Install a backdoor, delete the database, pivot to every other system on the network, mine crypto, encrypt everything for ransom
The server after	Still running, still "normal" — you just peeked at a file	Yours. It's doing what you say.
Analogy	I walked into the back office and read a file off the desk	I walked into the back office and took over the building

Directory traversal is a step toward RCE. If you traverse to a config file and find database credentials, you can now log in as the database admin. If you traverse to source code and find an RCE vulnerability, you now have RCE. But traversal alone is just espionage. RCE is full control.

Exam line: Traversal leaks information. RCE gives you a foothold. Information can be rotated; a foothold means you're in.
