Topic 10: Overview: Mobile Device Vulnerabilities 2.3

The big idea: Phones come with built-in guard rails. The official app store checks apps before you can install them, and the operating system keeps each app in its own “room” so it can’t interfere with other apps or the system. Most mobile vulnerabilities come from removing those guard rails.

1. Sideloading
Installing an app from outside the official store, for example downloading an app file from a website or getting one sent to you. Nobody has checked it for malware. It’s like buying medicine from a stranger’s car boot instead of a pharmacy. It might be fine, but there’s no quality control. Android allows sideloading more easily. Apple has traditionally blocked it, though rules are loosening in some countries.

2. Jailbreaking (iPhone) and rooting (Android)
Deliberately breaking the phone’s built-in restrictions to get full control. This lets people install anything and change system settings. But it also removes the walls that keep malware contained, so a bad app could reach everything. It’s like taking out every internal door and lock in your house because they’re inconvenient. Jailbroken phones may also stop receiving security updates.

3. Why businesses care
If a jailbroken or rooted phone has work email, files or company apps on it, the risk spreads to the whole organisation. That’s why workplaces use MDM (Mobile Device Management). It can detect jailbroken or rooted phones, block them from company data, enforce passcodes, and even wipe a lost phone remotely.


What’s the difference between sideloading and jailbreaking/rooting?

Why would a company block a rooted phone from accessing work email? 
CompTIA Security+ 2.3: Mobile Device Vulnerabilities

Phones come with built-in guard rails. The official app store checks apps before you install them, and the operating system keeps each app in its own space so it can’t interfere with other apps or the system. Most mobile vulnerabilities come from removing those guard rails, either by accident or on purpose.

Sideloading means installing apps from outside the official app store. Nobody has checked these apps for malware. It’s like buying medicine from a stranger’s car boot instead of a pharmacy. I’ve seen this first-hand with someone I care for who isn’t confident with technology. He’d see an ad on Facebook promising money or an investment return, tap it, and land on a website warning that his phone “needed cleaning.” He’d tap install, more pop-ups would appear, he’d install more, and soon his phone was stuck in a loop he couldn’t escape. Some of this was probably sideloaded malware or adware. Some may have been browser notification spam, where a scam site tricks you into tapping “Allow” and then floods your phone with fake alerts. Two Android settings help prevent this: switch “Install unknown apps” off for browsers and Facebook, and remove any unrecognised sites from Chrome’s notification settings.

How I handled it. Twice, I did a full factory reset, because when you can’t be sure a device is clean, wiping it is the only safe option. In security terms, that’s remediation. After the first time, I set it up so nothing could be installed without my PIN. After the second time, I set up parental controls so every download needs my approval. That’s essentially a home version of MDM (Mobile Device Management), which businesses use to control what goes on work phones. In future when I get a new modem I will setup a guest WIFI so that it doesn't cause a problem for my network. This is segmentation, keeping evey part secure so it cant creep or pivot into my network.
Right now, everything shares one network, so any device on it can potentially “see” and reach every other device, including your laptop. A guest network gives a guest a key to the front gate only. He can get out to the street (the internet), but there’s no internal door into the house.

In a business, the idea is the same, just bigger. From Network+:

VLANs divide one physical network into separate virtual networks: staff, guests, printers, security cameras and so on.
Separate SSIDs are often linked to each VLAN. That’s why an office might have “Company-Staff” and “Company-Guest” Wi-Fi.
Firewall rules or ACLs between the segments control what’s allowed to cross. Guests usually get internet only, with no access to internal systems.

Many guest networks also have client isolation, which stops guest devices from even seeing each other. So if one guest’s phone is infected, it can’t reach another guest’s phone either. The main trade-off is that devices on the guest network usually can’t reach things on your main network, like a shared printer or a casting device

Jailbreaking (iPhone) and rooting (Android) are different. They’re deliberate. The owner breaks the phone’s built-in restrictions to get full control, so they can install anything and change system settings. But this also removes the protections that keep malware contained. It’s like taking out every internal door and lock in your house because they’re inconvenient. Installing updates often undoes a jailbreak, so people avoid updating, which leaves the phone unpatched and exposed. This is different from turning on Android’s Developer options, which are built in and don’t remove the phone’s security protections (though it’s wise to switch off USB debugging when you’re not using it).

Why businesses care. If an employee’s jailbroken or rooted phone has work email, files or apps on it, the risk spreads to the whole company, because an attacker could use that phone as a way back into the network. That’s why companies use MDM to detect and block these phones. I apply the same thinking at home: I don’t want a possibly compromised phone sharing my Wi-Fi with my own devices. Putting untrusted devices on a separate guest network is a simple form of network segmentation. My own modem is 8 to 10 years old and may not support it, which is a reminder that old hardware is a vulnerability too. Once a device stops receiving firmware updates, it’s end-of-life, and any new flaws will never be fixed.
