Topic Operating System 2.3 
Overview: Operating System Vulnerabilities

The big idea: An operating system like Windows, macOS or Linux is enormous, with tens of millions of lines of code written by thousands of people. With that much code, mistakes are guaranteed. Some of those mistakes become security holes. And because the OS sits underneath everything on the computer, a flaw there can give an attacker control of the whole machine.

Think of the OS as the foundations and frame of a house. A faulty lock on one door is bad. A crack in the foundations affects every room.

Key points:

1. Flaws are found constantly
This isn’t a rare event. New OS vulnerabilities are discovered every month, by vendors, security researchers and attackers. That’s why every major OS has a regular update cycle.

2. Patch Tuesday
Microsoft releases its security fixes on the second Tuesday of each month. Each month’s release often fixes dozens of vulnerabilities at once, and some of them are rated critical, meaning an attacker could take control of the computer remotely.

3. The danger window
Once a patch is released, attackers study it to work out what was broken, then go after everyone who hasn’t installed it yet. So the period after a patch comes out, before organisations install it, is when unpatched systems are most at risk. Sometimes this is described as “Patch Tuesday, Exploit Wednesday.”

4. Best practices for patching BACK UP YOUR DATA Having a OS backup image using Rufus like for my Ubuntu server is a good idea. 

Patch regularly and promptly. Don’t let updates pile up
.
Test first. In a business, updates are tested on a few machines before rolling out to everyone, because a patch can occasionally break an important application.
Have a fallback plan. Take backups and know how to roll back an update if it causes problems.
Automate where possible. Tools like WSUS, Intune or other patch management systems push updates to many computers at once.
Why operating systems are a big target?
find one flaw, and you’ve found it for millions of machines. That scale is exactly what makes OS flaws so valuable. One working exploit can be used against every unpatched computer running that OS.

The risk after a patch?
Attackers reverse-engineer the patch to work out what was broken.
People delay updates. A day becomes a week, and that’s a week of exposure. “Patch Tuesday, Exploit Wednesday” is exactly right.

Why test before rolling out?
one bad patch pushed to everyone at once could take down mortgages, cards, the whole bank.

Pilot group or phased rollout: patch a small group first, check nothing breaks, then expand.
Test environment: ideally, patches are tested on non-production machines before going anywhere near real users.

The pivoting point needs separating out. Two different risks got blended together:
Rollout risk: a bad patch breaks a whole clinic at once. Doing one or two machines first reduces this.
Exposure risk: any machine still waiting for the patch is vulnerable, and an attacker could pivot between them.
Every phased rollout has this trade-off: going slowly is safer for stability, but leaves machines exposed for longer. Businesses balance it by patching the most critical and internet-facing systems first, and by segmenting networks, like keeping clinics separate, so one compromised machine can’t easily reach the rest.

Overview: Malicious Updates 2.3

The big idea: We’re always told to install updates, and for good reason. But what if the update itself is the attack? A malicious update is malware delivered through a legitimate update channel. Because it comes from a trusted source, it looks normal, passes the usual checks, and gets installed by everyone.

It’s like a trusted delivery company bringing you a parcel that someone tampered with at the depot. You’d never suspect it, because you know the driver.

How it happens:

Compromising the vendor: attackers break into the software company’s systems, often the servers that build or distribute updates, and slip their code into a genuine update before it’s released.
Real digital signatures: because the update comes out of the vendor’s own system, it’s signed by the real vendor. The signature proves who it came from, but not that the vendor’s systems were clean. This links back to digital signatures in 1.4.
Fake update prompts: the less sophisticated version is a pop-up like “Your browser is out of date, click to update” on a dodgy website. That’s closer to what happened with Al’s phone.

A famous example: SolarWinds (2020).
Attackers broke into SolarWinds, whose network monitoring software is used by thousands of organisations, including US government agencies. They hid a backdoor inside a genuine software update. Around 18,000 customers installed it, and it went undetected for months. It was attributed to a nation-state, which links back to 2.1. It’s also a supply chain attack, which links to 2.2 and the next video.

How to defend against it:

Only download updates from the official source, never from pop-ups or links.
Test updates on a small group before rolling them out widely (your phased rollout point from the OS topic).
Monitor for unusual behaviour after updates, since a valid signature isn’t proof of safety.
Vendors must protect their own build systems, because they’re a prime target.

The tension to notice: you’re told to patch quickly, but a patch could be the attack. That’s why testing and monitoring matter, not just speed.

Why is a malicious update so hard to spot? If an update is properly digitally signed, why isn’t that enough to prove it’s safe?
